# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ATC — Atlantic Core  |  VMP server.cfg example
# Copy to your VMP server root as server.cfg and fill in the values.
#
# VMP (vmp.ir) is a CitizenFX-compatible platform — it is a fork of the FiveM
# server, so the resource contract is the same one FiveM uses: fxmanifest.lua,
# fx_version 'cerulean', game 'gta5', resources identified by folder name,
# the same start/ensure commands, the same sv_* convars and the same natives.
#
# ATC therefore runs on VMP UNMODIFIED. No resource in this repo needs porting.
# Only the deployment plumbing differs, and everything that differs is in this
# file. The FiveM variant is infra/server.cfg.example; the ATC convar block
# below is identical in both.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Server identity ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Shown on the VMP server list. Replace it with your own.
# It is NOT the name ATC shows in-game — see "Server branding" below and set
# atc_brand_name to match, otherwise your listing and your in-game UI disagree.
sv_hostname "Your Server Name — [TAG]"
sv_maxclients 64
sets tags "atc,roleplay,mmo"

# ── License ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A VMP licence key is MANDATORY. This is verified in VMP's server source, not
# assumed: the startup licence check calls FatalError on every failure path,
# which stops the server (ServerAuth.cpp:30-80).
#
# The key is validated against VMP's OWN licensing service at https://api.vmp.ir/
# (ServerLicensingComponent.h:36) — NOT against keymaster.fivem.net. A FiveM key
# will never authenticate here, and a VMP key will never authenticate on FiveM.
#
# At startup the server POSTs {"license":"<your key>"} to
#   https://api.vmp.ir/server/register.php?work=register        (ServerAuth.cpp:44)
# and on success prints "Server license key authentication succeeded!" plus your
# Session Id.
#
# The three failure messages, so you can tell them apart:
#   "Please set sv_licenseKey in server.cfg!"                   -> convar empty
#   "A connection with the VMP server could not be established!"-> api.vmp.ir unreachable
#   (a message from VMP's API, verbatim)                        -> key rejected
#
# IPv4 IS REQUIRED: both the licence request (ServerAuth.cpp:43) and the list
# heartbeat (GameServer.cpp:1001) force IPv4. A host with only IPv6 internet
# connectivity cannot license or get listed.
#
# Where to get a key: that procedure is published only on VMP's website and
# community forum, which are not reachable from outside Iran — obtain it from
# VMP directly. What is certain is the endpoint it will be checked against.
sv_licenseKey "YOUR_VMP_LICENSE_KEY"

# Do NOT set sv_sessionId or sv_secret by hand. The licensing handshake writes
# both from VMP's response, and the heartbeat explicitly refuses to send while
# either one is empty (GameServer.cpp:971-973). Setting them manually will not
# make an unlicensed server appear on the list.

# ── Server list ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Listing uses the standard FXServer heartbeat. sv_master1 already defaults to
#   https://api.vmp.ir/server/heartbeat.php?work=heartbeat  (GameServer.cpp:54,120)
# so you do NOT configure it. The heartbeat repeats every 3 minutes.
#
# A valid licence is what gets you listed: the heartbeat returns immediately
# while sv_sessionId or sv_secret is empty, and only a successful licence
# handshake fills those in.
#
# Leave sv_master1 alone — and note the failure is SILENT. The heartbeat carries
# a "private" flag that starts as true and is set to false only when one of
# sv_master1..sv_master3 is EXACTLY the default VMP URL (GameServer.cpp:1039-1070).
# Point sv_master1 elsewhere and the heartbeat still fires — your server just
# stays flagged private and never appears publicly. Do not point it at a FiveM
# master; that master does not know about this server.
#
# If your server is behind NAT, a proxy or a load balancer and it lists with the
# wrong address, use the standard overrides (same names and meanings as FiveM):
# set sv_listingIpOverride "your.public.ip"
# set sv_listingHostOverride "play.yourserver.example"
# set sv_forceIndirectListing "true"
#
# If your server still does not appear with a valid key, a running heartbeat and
# a correct hostname, ask on the VMP community forum — that is where operators
# raise listing problems, and whatever additional steps VMP may require are
# theirs to state, not ours to guess.
#
# Players join with the VMP launcher, downloaded from VMP's website, not with
# the FiveM client. Joining is done through the launcher, from VMP's server list
# or its direct-connect field; use whatever address format the launcher itself
# asks for.

# ── Platform ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which CitizenFX platform this server runs on: auto | fivem | vmp | redm
# Pinned to "vmp" here so ATC's logs, telemetry and ops records name the right
# platform even if runtime detection cannot tell this fork apart from upstream.
# Set it back to "auto" if you move this config to a FiveM host.
# ATC never branches gameplay on this — it affects reporting only.
# Do not set atc_platform_resolved — the server writes that one itself from the
# detection result and replicates it to clients.
set atc_platform "vmp"

# ── Server branding (self-hosting) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything a player sees with your community's name on it is set here, in one
# place: the NUI logo, the tag on connect/kick/ban messages and the tutorial
# welcome copy.
#
# The WEB admin panel (apps/web) is NOT covered by these convars. It is a
# separately built React app and a browser cannot read game-server convars; its
# name comes from the build-time VITE_BRAND_NAME environment variable instead.
#
# Every convar below is optional. Leave one unset and ATC uses the default shown
# beside it, which is exactly the string the framework shipped before these
# convars existed — a deployment that sets none of them behaves as before.
#
# ATC, Atlantic Core and Naiemi Group stay as the framework's attribution (see
# LICENSE): the fxmanifests, the docs and the startup log keep them. These
# convars change what your players see, not who wrote the code.
#
# Note the "setr" below — it is deliberate, not a typo. Branding is read by
# client-side script (the tutorial and the NUI), and a plain "set" convar stays
# on the server and never reaches the client. Using "set" here leaves the
# in-game UI showing the shipped defaults while the server log shows your name.
# Every other ATC convar in this file stays "set": those are server-only, and
# atc_api_token / atc_server_token MUST never be made replicated.

# Community / server name, up to 64 characters. Shown in the in-game tutorial
# and on the character screens.
# Keep this in sync with sv_hostname above. They are two different things:
# sv_hostname is your entry in the server browser, atc_brand_name is the same
# name rendered inside the game. Setting one and not the other is the usual way
# a rebranded server ends up half-renamed.
# Default: "Atlantic Core"
# setr atc_brand_name "Your Server Name"

# Short tag, up to 16 characters, used as the prefix on connection, kick and ban
# messages — e.g. "[ATC] You are banned from this server."
# Default: "ATC"
# setr atc_brand_short "TAG"

# The two-word logo on the character-select screen, up to 16 characters each.
# The first word is drawn in the accent colour, the second in light weight.
# Uppercase reads best.
# Defaults: "ATLANTIC" and "CORE"
# setr atc_brand_logo_primary   "YOUR"
# setr atc_brand_logo_secondary "SERVER"

# Optional community line, website and Discord invite. All three default to
# empty, and empty stays empty. They are sanitised and delivered to the NUI with
# the rest of the branding payload, but atc-core's own interface renders NO
# element for any of them today — setting them changes nothing visible in the
# stock UI. They exist for plugins and custom UI to consume.
# Defaults: "" (unset)
# setr atc_brand_community "Your Community"
# setr atc_brand_website   "https://example.com"
# setr atc_brand_discord   "https://discord.gg/your-invite"

# Accent colour used throughout the NUI, as a 6-digit hex triplet with a leading
# "#". Anything that is not a valid triplet is ignored and the default is kept.
# Default: "#d4af37"
# setr atc_brand_color "#d4af37"

# ── ATC Core convars (REQUIRED) ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# URL where the ATC API is running (must be reachable from the game server)
set atc_api_url "http://localhost:3000"

# API bearer token — must match ATC_API_TOKEN in infra/.env
set atc_api_token "changeme_api_token_min32chars"

# Server token for inter-service authentication
set atc_server_token "changeme_server_token"

# Unique server identifier (used for multi-server setups)
set atc_server_id "atc-main-01"

# Default locale: en | de | fa
set atc_locale "en"

# Fail-open: if true players can join even when the API is unreachable
# false = safer default (block on API failure)
set atc_fail_open "false"

# API request timeout in milliseconds
set atc_api_timeout_ms "5000"

# Debug logging (verbose — disable in production)
set atc_debug "false"

# ── ATC Admin ACE (required for /atckick, /atcban, /atcbring, etc.) ───────────
# VMP exposes the same identity providers as FiveM — license, steam and
# endpoint/ip — and ATC keys every player record on the "license" identifier,
# so granting admin by license is the path that always works. Replace the
# placeholder with your own license identifier (find it in the server console
# when you connect).
add_ace identifier.license:XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX atc.admin allow

# Alternative: grant by Steam hex identifier instead. Only works for players who
# have Steam running and linked.
# add_ace identifier.steam:XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX atc.admin allow

# ── Resources ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Resources are started by FOLDER name, exactly as on FiveM. The `name` field
# inside fxmanifest.lua is metadata only — it does not rename the resource. Keep
# the ATC folder names exactly as they ship: the NUI pages address themselves as
# https://<folder>/ and renaming a folder breaks its UI.

# Core (must be first)
start atc-core

# SDK (required for external plugins)
start atc-sdk

# First-party plugins (order matters — identity before others)
start atc-identity
start atc-inventory
start atc-economy
start atc-housing
start atc-vehicles
start atc-jobs
start atc-combat
start atc-territory
start atc-dispatch
start atc-admin
start atc-phone
start atc-mdt
start atc-ems
start atc-criminal
start atc-marketplace

# Reference / example plugin (optional — demonstrates ATC SDK usage)
# start atc-example-shop

# NOTE: plugins/atc-plugin-healthcheck is deliberately absent from this list.
# It has no fxmanifest.lua — it is a server-side Node plugin for the ATC API,
# not a game resource. Adding a start line for it produces a "resource not
# found" error.

# ── Compatibility bridges (optional — only if you already run ESX or QBCore) ──
# Read this before uncommenting anything: in this repo the bridge FOLDER names
# and the `name` in their fxmanifest.lua do not match.
#
#     bridges/esx/fxmanifest.lua      says  name 'atc-bridge-esx'
#     bridges/qb-core/fxmanifest.lua  says  name 'atc-bridge-qb'
#
# Since the server starts resources by folder name, copying the folders across
# unchanged means you must start them as "esx" and "qb-core".
#
# Do NOT do that for the QBCore bridge: a QBCore server already has a resource
# directory called qb-core, and dropping this one alongside it collides with the
# very framework the bridge exists to talk to.
#
# Recommended: rename each folder as you copy it, so folder, manifest name and
# start line all agree —
#
#     bridges/esx      ->  resources/[atc]/atc-bridge-esx
#     bridges/qb-core  ->  resources/[atc]/atc-bridge-qb
#
# then uncomment these:
# start atc-bridge-esx
# start atc-bridge-qb
#
# If you did copy the folders without renaming, use these instead:
# start esx
# start qb-core

# ── VMP deployment notes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server artifacts: VMP publishes its own server builds. They are NOT published
#   as GitHub releases — VMP's public GitHub repository has none, and its build
#   pipeline runs on infrastructure of its own. A FiveM artifact will not accept
#   a VMP licence key. The on-disk shape is the same as FXServer's: a
#   server.zip / server.7z on Windows, an fx.tar.xz on Linux, the binary still
#   named FXServer / FXServer.exe, started with `FXServer.exe +exec server.cfg`
#   or `./run.sh +exec server.cfg`. Get the current build and its download
#   layout from what VMP publishes.
#
# Player client: players use the VMP launcher, downloaded from VMP's website —
#   not the FiveM client.
#
# Third-party assets: encrypted Cfx.re "escrow" assets are decrypted against
#   Cfx.re's own service, so it would be unwise to count on them working on a
#   non-Cfx.re platform — but we did not verify what VMP actually does with
#   them. Plan for open-source or unencrypted resources alongside ATC, and test
#   before you rely on anything escrowed. ATC itself ships no escrowed assets,
#   so this never affects ATC's own resources.
#
# Compatibility: every ATC resource uses only stock CitizenFX natives, exports
#   and events, which VMP provides unchanged. If a THIRD-PARTY resource
#   misbehaves on VMP, that is between you and that resource — ask on the VMP
#   community forum, where operators discuss which scripts need adjusting.
